  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nAs of 18z, there are scattered afternoon cumulus clouds developing \nover central and northeastern SD. Instability values of 2000-3000 \nJ/kg this afternoon as well as a lot of moisture in the atmosphere \nwill help with storm development this evening into the overnight \nhours. The main concern with the developing storms will be if the \nweak forcing can get the storms to develop and stay alive into the \nnight. High-res models are showing the storms developing off of the \nLee of the Rockies surface trough in western SD late this afternoon \ninto the evening and then moving to the east/southeast into central \nSD this evening into early overnight hours. The next question is if \nthere will be enough forcing to support the storms into the \novernight hours as they try to move through central and portions of \nnortheastern SD. This will come down to if the low level jet, as \nsome models have the jet develop and then support the storms through \nthe overnight hours while other models keep the jet from developing \nand then the storms die off during the late evening. The models are \nalso showing the environment over central and northeastern SD has \nelongated hodographs, which hint that the storms will become \nsplitting supercell. The splitting supercells are known to produce \nlarge hail, and with the stronger lapse rates, there could be hail \nup around 2 inches. Stronger winds are also possible, especially if \nthe low level jet develop, with wind gusts of 60-70 mph possible. \nThere is a slight risk (level 2 of 5) over central SD for storms to \nbecome severe if they develop and a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) \nover northeastern SD for severe storms if they move into the area. \n\nThe models are also hinting at some storms developing in ND early \nWednesday morning that could then move southeast into north central \nor northeastern SD. There is currently lower confidence on this due \nto the variability in the models, mainly from if there are stronger \nstorms this evening that will decrease the instability and stop the \nstorms in the morning, or if there are not storms that happen then \nthere will be more instability to support the storms in the morning. \nThrough Wednesday, the instability will be building over central and \nnortheastern SD. More storms are forecast to develop along the Lee \nof the Rockies surface trough in western SD and ND Wednesday \nafternoon and then move east and southeast through central and \nnortheastern SD during the late afternoon and evening. The NAM is \nshowing stronger shear values with these storms, but not very strong \nlapse rates. This hints that very large hail will not be a large \nthreat. However, there is still a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for \nthe storms to become severe with hail up to 1 inch and wind gusts of \n60mph. \n\nThere will continue to be wildfire smoke aloft over central and \nnortheastern SD through today and into Wednesday. While this smoke \nwill likely only cause hazy skies across the area, there are some \nareas east of the Missouri River that could get a bit of this smoke \nto come to the surface, which could cause some problems to those who \nare sensitive. Temperatures will be coolings slightly Wednesday, \nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Wednesday night, colder \ntemperatures will be pushing in behind the the storms that move \nthrough. This will help to cool the temperatures through the end of \nthe week into the weekend to be 5-10 degrees cooler than normal. \nHighs will be in the 70s during this time.",
